    {
      "id": "4ef355c8-5b85-4be7-a94f-6d02286ed3ae",
      "phaseId": "3e04a8f5-3c51-4f2d-8fab-d51b392e80bc",
      "number": 296,
      "shortId": "8S9M6",
      "priority": 5,
      "shortName": "action-task-d-migrate-built-in",
      "title": "action-task-D — Migrate built-ins onto register(ActionSpec)",
      "status": "canceled",
      "description": "Follow-up MECCANICO di action-task-C (2026-07-15). Puramente opzionale: nessun cambiamento funzionale, nessun bug — è cleanup/unificazione.\n\n# ⚠️ MEASURED 2026-07-27 — THE PREMISE DOES NOT HOLD, AND THE SAFETY GOAL IS ALREADY MET\n\n## The two lists do not pair 1:1\n\n**148 descriptors in `ActionRegistry::ALL`, but only 132 distinct `WmAction` variants with a registered handler.** A handler is keyed by the enum **variant**, not by the name, and several names share one variant. Verified example (actions.rs:820/829/838 vs registry.rs:725):\n\n| Name — 3 palette entries, 3 keybindings | Variant — 1 handler |\n|---|---|\n| `app_font_increase` | `WmAction::AppFontZoom { step: In }` |\n| `app_font_decrease` | `WmAction::AppFontZoom { step: Out }` |\n| `app_font_reset` | `WmAction::AppFontZoom { step: Reset }` |\n\nOne `ActionSpec` per NAME therefore means writing the same handler 16 times over, registering it repeatedly onto the same discriminant key (later calls overwriting earlier ones — harmless, but pointless). **\"One place per action, so they cannot drift\" is true for the 132 that are 1:1 and quietly false for the other 16.**\n\n## What it would cost\n\n- **More code, not less.** A 148-entry const array + a 132-line `build_registry()` becomes 148 larger literals.\n- **A hand-pairing job.** Each literal needs the right `handle_*` function beside it. One wrong pair silently binds a key to the wrong action — the exact class of silent failure that F003/P010/T006, F003/P017/T005 and F003/P017/T006 were all spent removing.\n\n## THE SAFETY GOAL IS DONE (commits a413730 + d95cde20)\n\nThe task's stated purpose — \"togliere la possibilità di drift ALL↔handler\" — is enforced by two tests closing both directions:\n\n- **`every_catalogued_action_has_a_handler`** (heca/src/app/registry.rs) — walks all 148 descriptors, resolves each to its `WmAction` (by name, or built from its **declared `args`**), asserts a handler is registered. Verified by deleting one `register()` line: it fails and names `zoom_column`.\n- **`every_wm_action_variant_is_reachable_by_name`** (heca/src/input.rs, from F003/P010/T006) — the other direction: walks every variant, asserts a descriptor names it. This is the direction that had actually rotted — 23 argument-taking actions had handlers and no descriptor at all.\n\nDrift is now *caught in the same commit that causes it* rather than *impossible*. That is a smaller difference than it sounds.\n\n## WHAT IS ACTUALLY LEFT HERE\n\nUniformity of shape only: one way to register an action instead of two, so a reader learns one pattern. Genuine but small — and **not** a capability question, see below.\n\n## ⚠️ IT IS NOT ABOUT PLUGINS. THE DYNAMIC PATH ALREADY EXISTS\n\nThe user asked (2026-07-27) whether a plugin must edit `build_registry` to add an action. **No.** `register_dynamic(registry, catalog, meta, handler)` registers a name-keyed action at runtime — no enum variant, no `build_registry` edit, no rebuild. It joins the same one catalog, so it gets a label, an icon, `list-actions`, `describe-action` (including its declared `args`), menu dispatch, RPC, and config binding by name — the last even *before* it exists, since config is read before providers mount and an unresolved name resolves at press time. `handler: None` registers a `Dispatch::Declarative` action, forwarded to its owner across the WASM boundary.\n\n`build_registry()` is the **built-in** path only. Built-ins keep the enum deliberately: discriminant dispatch is zero-cost, and `action_policy()`'s exhaustive match makes forgetting to classify a new one a compile error.\n\n**Still missing, and it is not this task:** nothing in production calls `register_dynamic` yet — it carries `expect(dead_code, reason = \"plugin-04 seam: the first registrant is T1 (chrome placement actions) / a provider; exercised by tests today\")`. Architecturally a plugin can add an action; demonstrably nobody has. That belongs to F003/P001 (WASM runtime) and F003/P008.\n\n## WHEN THIS WOULD BECOME WORTH DOING\n\nIf built-ins ever need registering at **runtime** — conditional on platform, unloadable, or if the `WmAction` enum is retired. None is on the table; if one arrives, the merge comes with it for free.\n\n═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nORIGINAL DESCRIPTION (2026-07-15) — the plan as first written\n═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\n## STATO ATTUALE\nI ~115 built-in prendono i metadata da `ActionRegistry::ALL` (const array in `heca/src/actions.rs`), seedati nel catalogo da `ActionCatalog::with_builtins()`, e gli handler da `build_registry()` (`heca/src/app/registry.rs`). Sono DUE punti separati che possono divergere (un descriptor in ALL senza handler, o viceversa).\n\naction-task-C ha aggiunto l'API nativa a una chiamata: `register(registry, catalog, ActionSpec { action, handler, meta })` (`heca/src/actions.rs`) che cabla handler (per discriminante) + meta insieme. Ma i built-in NON sono stati migrati — l'API esiste e i built-in usano ancora il vecchio doppio path.\n\n## LAVORO (se/quando si vuole fare)\n1. Sostituire il seeding `const ALL` + `build_registry()` con una serie di `register(ActionSpec { ... })`, una per azione, così handler e metadata stanno in UN posto per azione e non possono divergere.\n2. `ActionRegistry::ALL` può poi essere ritirato (o ridotto), come previsto da action-interaction-plan §5.1.\n3. Attaccare i 3 confirm spec (close/delete_column/delete_workspace) direttamente nel loro `ActionSpec.meta.confirm`, eliminando il fixup post-seed in `with_builtins`.\n4. Gate invariato: clippy 0 warning; `cargo test -p heca`; niente `cargo fmt`.\n\n## PERCHÉ È A BASSA PRIORITÀ\nZero guadagno funzionale: il catalogo prodotto è identico. Il solo vantaggio è togliere la possibilità di drift ALL↔handler e avere un solo modo di registrare un'azione. Farlo quando conviene toccare comunque quella zona.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [
        {
          "id": "4ef355c8-5b85-4be7-a94f-6d02286ed3ae-check-001-in-progress-planned",
          "date": "2026-07-27T07:08:36.716Z",
          "fromStatus": "in-progress",
          "toStatus": "planned",
          "title": "Started 2026-07-27 and stopped before the rewrite: measuring the two lists showed the task's premise does not hold (148 names vs 132 handler-bearing variants, so they do not pair 1:1). The safety property the task exists for was delivered instead, by two guard tests. The structural merge is left planned because it is still a legitimate choice for uniformity — but it now buys shape, not safety, and the user has been given the numbers. Not started as specified; pending their call.</motivation>",
          "description": "Started 2026-07-27 and stopped before the rewrite: measuring the two lists showed the task's premise does not hold (148 names vs 132 handler-bearing variants, so they do not pair 1:1). The safety property the task exists for was delivered instead, by two guard tests. The structural merge is left planned because it is still a legitimate choice for uniformity — but it now buys shape, not safety, and the user has been given the numbers. Not started as specified; pending their call.</motivation>\n<parameter name=\"description\">Follow-up MECCANICO di action-task-C (2026-07-15). Puramente opzionale: nessun cambiamento funzionale, nessun bug — è cleanup/unificazione. NON è debito \"for now\" (il path esistente funziona); registrare qui perché la nota non resti sepolta in un task chiuso.\n\n# ⚠️ MEASURED 2026-07-27 — THE PREMISE DOES NOT HOLD, AND THE SAFETY GOAL IS ALREADY MET\n\n## The two lists do not pair 1:1\n\n**148 descriptors in `ActionRegistry::ALL`, but only 132 distinct `WmAction` variants with a registered handler.** A handler is keyed by the enum **variant**, not by the name, and several names share one variant. Verified example (actions.rs:820/829/838 vs registry.rs:725):\n\n| Name — 3 palette entries, 3 keybindings | Variant — 1 handler |\n|---|---|\n| `app_font_increase` | `WmAction::AppFontZoom { step: In }` |\n| `app_font_decrease` | `WmAction::AppFontZoom { step: Out }` |\n| `app_font_reset` | `WmAction::AppFontZoom { step: Reset }` |\n\nSo one `ActionSpec` per NAME means writing the same handler 16 times over, registering it repeatedly onto the same discriminant key (later calls overwriting earlier ones — harmless, but pointless). **\"One place per action, so they cannot drift\" is true for the 132 that are 1:1 and quietly false for the other 16.**\n\n## What it would cost\n\n- **More code, not less.** A 148-entry const array + a 132-line `build_registry()` becomes 148 larger literals.\n- **A hand-pairing job.** Each of the 148 literals needs the right `handle_*` function beside it. One wrong pair silently binds a key to the wrong action — the exact class of silent failure F003/P010/T006, F003/P017/T005 and F003/P017/T006 were all spent removing.\n\n## THE SAFETY GOAL IS DONE (commit d95cde20 + a413730)\n\nThe task's stated purpose — \"togliere la possibilità di drift ALL↔handler\" — is now enforced by two tests that close both directions:\n\n- **`every_catalogued_action_has_a_handler`** (heca/src/app/registry.rs) — walks all 148 descriptors, resolves each to its `WmAction` (by name, or built from its **declared `args`**), asserts a handler is registered. Verified by deleting one `register()` line: it fails and names `zoom_column`.\n- **`every_wm_action_variant_is_reachable_by_name`** (heca/src/input.rs, added by F003/P010/T006) — the other direction: walks every variant and asserts a descriptor names it. This is the direction that had actually rotted — 23 argument-taking actions had handlers and no descriptor at all.\n\nDrift is now *caught in the same commit that causes it*, rather than *impossible*. That is a smaller difference than it sounds.\n\n## WHAT IS ACTUALLY LEFT IN THIS TASK\n\nUniformity of shape only: one way to register an action instead of two, so a reader learns one pattern. Genuine but small, and it is **not** a capability question — see below.\n\n## ⚠️ IT IS NOT ABOUT PLUGINS. THE DYNAMIC PATH ALREADY EXISTS\n\nThe user asked (2026-07-27) whether a plugin has to edit `build_registry` to add an action. **No.** `register_dynamic(registry, catalog, meta, handler)` registers a name-keyed action at runtime — no enum variant, no `build_registry` edit, no rebuild. It joins the same one catalog, so it gets a label, an icon, `list-actions`, `describe-action` (including its declared `args`), menu dispatch, RPC, and config binding by name — the last even *before* it exists, since config is read before providers mount and an unresolved name resolves at press time. `handler: None` registers a `Dispatch::Declarative` action, forwarded to its owner across the WASM boundary.\n\n`build_registry()` is the **built-in** path only. Built-ins keep the enum deliberately: discriminant dispatch is zero-cost, and `action_policy()`'s exhaustive match makes forgetting to classify a new one a compile error.\n\n**Still missing, and it is not this task:** nothing in production calls `register_dynamic` yet — it carries `expect(dead_code, reason = \"plugin-04 seam: the first registrant is T1 (chrome placement actions) / a provider; exercised by tests today\")`. Architecturally a plugin can add an action; demonstrably nobody has. That belongs to F003/P001 (WASM runtime) and F003/P008.\n\n## WHEN THIS WOULD BECOME WORTH DOING\n\nIf built-ins ever need registering at **runtime** — conditional on platform, unloadable, or if the `WmAction` enum is retired. None of those is on the table; if one arrives, the merge comes with it for free.\n\n═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nORIGINAL DESCRIPTION (2026-07-15) BELOW — the plan as first written\n═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\n## STATO ATTUALE\nI ~115 built-in prendono i metadata da `ActionRegistry::ALL` (const array in `heca/src/actions.rs`), seedati nel catalogo da `ActionCatalog::with_builtins()`, e gli handler da `build_registry()` (`heca/src/app/registry.rs`). Sono DUE punti separati che possono divergere (un descriptor in ALL senza handler, o viceversa).\n\naction-task-C ha aggiunto l'API nativa a una chiamata: `register(registry, catalog, ActionSpec { action, handler, meta })` (`heca/src/actions.rs`) che cabla handler (per discriminante) + meta insieme. Ma i built-in NON sono stati migrati — l'API esiste e i built-in usano ancora il vecchio doppio path.\n\n## LAVORO (se/quando si vuole fare)\n1. Sostituire il seeding `const ALL` + `build_registry()` con una serie di `register(ActionSpec { ... })`, una per azione, così handler e metadata stanno in UN posto per azione e non possono divergere.\n2. `ActionRegistry::ALL` può poi essere ritirato (o ridotto), come previsto da action-interaction-plan §5.1 (\"i dati escono da const ALL e finiscono in register_builtins()\").\n3. Attaccare i 3 confirm spec (close/delete_column/delete_workspace) direttamente nel loro `ActionSpec.meta.confirm`, eliminando il fixup post-seed in `with_builtins`.\n4. Gate invariato: `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features` 0 warning; `cargo test -p heca` (parità del catalogo: il test `builtin_metadata_survives_the_owned_ripple_unchanged` deve restare verde); niente `cargo fmt`.\n\n## PERCHÉ È A BASSA PRIORITÀ\nZero guadagno funzionale: il catalogo prodotto è identico. Il solo vantaggio è togliere la possibilità di drift ALL↔handler e avere un solo modo di registrare un'azione (nativo = dinamico). Farlo quando conviene toccare comunque quella zona."
        },
        {
          "id": "4ef355c8-5b85-4be7-a94f-6d02286ed3ae-check-002-planned-canceled",
          "date": "2026-07-27T07:10:53.066Z",
          "fromStatus": "planned",
          "toStatus": "canceled",
          "title": "Closed 2026-07-27 by the user's decision: \"if this task doesn't produce anything I want to close and not plan again. we need to close tasks and not keeping delaying.\"",
          "description": "Closed 2026-07-27 by the user's decision: \"if this task doesn't produce anything I want to close and not plan again. we need to close tasks and not keeping delaying.\"\n\nCanceled rather than done, because the specified work — 148 `register(ActionSpec)` literals replacing `const ALL` + `build_registry()` — was deliberately NOT carried out. Marking it done would be false.\n\nWHAT IT DID PRODUCE, and why the rest is not coming back:\n- The task's stated purpose was \"togliere la possibilità di drift ALL↔handler\". That is DONE, by test rather than by structure: `every_catalogued_action_has_a_handler` (heca/src/app/registry.rs, commit d95cde20) and `every_wm_action_variant_is_reachable_by_name` (heca/src/input.rs, commit a413730) close both directions. Deleting one `register()` line fails the first and names the action.\n- What remained after that was uniformity of shape only, and measuring killed it: 148 names vs 132 handler-bearing variants, so they do not pair 1:1 (`app_font_increase`/`_decrease`/`_reset` all share `WmAction::AppFontZoom`). The rewrite would have produced more code than it removed, repeated the same handler 16 times, and required hand-pairing 148 literals where one wrong pair silently binds a key to the wrong action.\n- It is NOT a plugin capability question. `register_dynamic` already lets a provider or plugin add an action at runtime with no enum variant and no `build_registry` edit. `build_registry()` is the built-in path only.\n\nDO NOT RE-PLAN THIS. If built-ins ever need registering at runtime — conditional on platform, unloadable, or the `WmAction` enum retired — the merge comes with that work for free and does not need a task of its own. The full measurements and reasoning stay in the description above."
        }
      ],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
